jq values are shared between the query's worker and the main thread, so that
outputs need not be copied out of the worker. jq counts references with plain
increments and fills three caches the first time a value is read: a literal
number's double and its text, and a string's hash. the counts become atomic,
the double and the hash are stored atomically, the hash before the flag that
says it is there, and the text is published with a compare-and-swap, the
loser freeing its own copy.

